# Stigmer Documentation Standards

**These standards are MANDATORY. Every file in `docs/` must conform.**

Full standards: `docs/standards/documentation-standards.md`
Terminology dictionary: `docs/standards/terminology.json`
Information architecture: `docs/standards/information-architecture.md`
Templates: `docs/standards/templates/`
Audience mindset: `_reminders/007_documentation-for-platform-builders.md`

---

## Audience

Stigmer documentation is written for **platform builders** who want to embed AI agent execution into their products. They are technically skilled, new to Stigmer, evaluating alternatives, and time-constrained.

Every page must move the reader closer to running agents in their platform. Content is organized around the Diataxis framework: Tutorials (quickstarts), How-to Guides (guides), Reference (CLI/SDK), and Explanation (concepts). Each document belongs to exactly one quadrant.

---

## Content Type Detection

The file path determines the content type and its governing template:

| Path prefix | Content type | Template |
|---|---|---|
| `docs/quickstarts/` | Quickstart | `templates/quickstart.mdx` |
| `docs/concepts/` | Concept | `templates/concept.mdx` |
| `docs/guides/` | How-to guide | `templates/how-to-guide.mdx` |
| `docs/cli/` | CLI reference | `templates/cli-reference.mdx` |
| `docs/sdk/` | SDK guide | `templates/sdk-guide.mdx` |
| `docs/architecture/` | Architecture | `templates/architecture.mdx` |
| `docs/adr/` | ADR | `templates/adr.mdx` |

Every document must follow the structure defined by its template. Do not invent new structures.

---

## Frontmatter

Required on every MDX file:

```yaml
---
title: "Page title"
description: "One-sentence summary, max 160 characters."
---
```

Optional: `sidebar_label`, `sidebar_position`, `tags`, `last_updated`.

---

## Heading Hierarchy

- Exactly one H1 (`#`) per document. Must match the `title` frontmatter field.
- H2 (`##`) for major sections. H3 (`###`) for subsections within an H2.
- Never skip levels (no H3 after H1).
- Sentence case: "The problem agents solve," not "The Problem Agents Solve."
- No code formatting in headings: "Install the CLI," not "Install \`stigmer\`."

---

## Code Blocks

- Every fenced code block MUST have a language tag (`yaml`, `bash`, `go`, `python`, `typescript`, `json`, `proto`, `mermaid`, `text`).
- YAML examples must be valid YAML, complete enough to `stigmer apply`.
- Bash examples must include expected output as a comment or separate `text` block.
- Go/Python/TypeScript examples must include imports when not obvious.

---

## Terminology

Proto names are canonical. The full dictionary is in `docs/standards/terminology.json`.

Key rules:
- `AgentExecution`, never "agent run" or "job"
- `McpServer`, never "tool connector"
- `Organization`, never "tenant" (exception: `--org` in CLI flags and YAML fields)
- `stigmer apply`, never "deploy" when referring to the CLI command
- `stigmer run`, never "execute" when referring to the CLI command

When in doubt, check `terminology.json`. If a term has an `exceptions` field, respect those contexts.

---

## Writing Style

- **Second person** ("you") when addressing the reader.
- **Present tense** for descriptions: "Stigmer creates an AgentInstance."
- **Imperative mood** for instructions: "Run the command."
- **Active voice** always: "Configure the environment," not "The environment should be configured."
- **No hedging**: Remove "usually," "sometimes," "might," "should probably." State conditions explicitly.
- **No filler**: Remove "basically," "simply," "just," "actually," "in order to."
- **No marketing language.** Documentation is not a press release.

---

## Cross-Referencing

- Use relative links that survive restructuring.
- Link to the canonical concept doc rather than re-explaining inline.
- Never duplicate explanations across documents.

---

## Diagrams

- Use Mermaid fenced code blocks for all diagrams — flowcharts, sequence diagrams, architecture diagrams, resource hierarchies, and pipelines.
- Never use `text` code blocks for visual structures like resource trees, architecture flows, or pipelines. If it has a visual structure, use Mermaid.
- Every diagram must have surrounding text that conveys the same information.

---

## Content Validation

- Every factual claim must be validated against the current codebase. Do not trust legacy documentation as a source.
- Proto definitions in `apis/ai/stigmer/` are the source of truth for domain concepts.
- CLI examples must be tested — run the command, verify the output.
- YAML examples must be valid and complete enough to use.
- Do not trust AI-generated content without verification against the implementation.
